### Start-of-let record

A start-of-let record logs each room’s state as a let starts: walls, floors, doors, glass, taps, oven, keys, and meter reads. Add dated photos.

At end of a let, compare the record and photos. Such a record helps show any harm beyond normal wear.

### Who holds copies

Landlord holds a master copy. Tenant gets a full copy at handover. An agent may hold a copy too. Have tenant add a date and a mark on each copy.

### What makes one weak at court

A court may see weak proof where:

- no date or dated photos;
- vague words, such as “good”;
- no room-by-room account;
- no key count or meter read;
- tenant lacks a copy;
- log made well after handover; or
- later changes lack dates and a clear note.

A cash backup policy is a short, written plan for money a charity holds back for hard times or major unknown costs.

It should say:

- why funds must stay put;
- how much is a right amount;
- risks that justify that amount;
- what might allow use of funds;
- who can authorise use; and
- how funds will build back up following use.

Why a watchdog asks about it

A watchdog wants proof that charity trustees look after charity funds with good judgment. A policy shows that a board has thought about what could go wrong, such as:

- gifts or grant funds arriving late;
- a grant running out;
- a fall in donations;
- a major fix or loss;
- an abrupt rise in costs; or
- a gap in cash flow.

It also shows that funds held back match actual risks, not an arbitrary sum. Too small a sum may put charity work at risk. Too high a sum may prompt a query as to why funds do not support charitable aims now.

How to draft it

1. List risks facing your charity.
2. Work out usual monthly running costs.
3. Map when cash is due in and when bills fall due.
4. Pick a target sum linked to your risks and costs.
5. Say who can authorise any drawdown.
6. Say how and when funds will return to target.
7. Ask trustees to review it annually, and following any big funding or cost shift.

No single sum fits all. A charity with long-term, solid funding may hold a small amount. A charity relying on short grants, gifts, or irregular cash may rationally hold a larger amount. What counts is a clear, written link among risk, cash flow, and funds held back.
